00:04:59.005 --> 00:04:59.880
What is this product?

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:00.960
What is it meant to do?

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:01.470
ERIC ANDERSON: Sure.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.136
Anyone who has worked
with data has felt

00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:04.345
the pain of data preparation.

00:05:04.345 --> 00:05:06.261
We talk a lot about all
the interesting things

00:05:06.261 --> 00:05:07.410
you can do with data--

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.320
visualization,
algorithms, analysis.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:13.760
And usually, we talk about it
on pretty nice and clean data.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.650
But in the real world, there's
friction, error resistance,

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:17.670
and there's bad data.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:19.980
And it's everywhere,
so Dataprep focuses on

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:24.270
that initial step used to do
any kind of data work, which

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:27.600
is to get your data in the
position in the structure

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.930
joined with the proper data
sets that you want so that you

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.500
can then go on and do analysis.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.000
So it's kind of a
top-of-the-funnel,

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.470
first-of-the-workflow product.

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:38.400
MARK MANDEL: And when
you say data or data

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:39.460
set, what does that mean?

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:41.040
Does that mean like,
I'm at home working

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.873
on my spreadsheet for
my taxes, or does that

00:05:42.873 --> 00:05:43.952
mean something else?

00:05:43.952 --> 00:05:45.660
ERIC ANDERSON: It
could, but really we're

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:48.030
talking generally
about large data

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:50.256
sets, what you might
define as big data.

00:05:50.256 --> 00:05:51.630
Presumably, these
are things that

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:55.480
are hard to see and find all
the errors just visually.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.956
And so, yeah, when you
can see all the data,

00:05:57.956 --> 00:06:00.330
then Dataprep might be the
thing to help you clean it up.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:01.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I'm trying
to understand a little bit

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:03.990
more, this is kind
of like a filter

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:06.840
to basically clean your data.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.300
And I understand that
concept, but what

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.250
does it look like to use it?

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:13.990
Data is a very generic thing.

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:17.040
What does it look to clean,
I don't know, phone numbers

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.622
or to clean my
credit card numbers.

00:06:19.622 --> 00:06:22.080
It's completely different, and
there's different algorithms

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:22.950
involved with that.

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:24.600
What does it mean
to clean the data?

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:25.433
ERIC ANDERSON: Sure.

00:06:25.433 --> 00:06:28.570
And I'll back up to give you
kind of a mental model and also

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:29.664
some mental images.

00:06:29.664 --> 00:06:31.080
You're familiar
with spreadsheets.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:31.890
Everyone is.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.110
And you can see your data.

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:34.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Unfortunately, yes.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:35.770
ERIC ANDERSON: Yeah.

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:38.010
So bad, but good also.

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:39.200
So good.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:43.620
So you've got a grid of
your data in a spreadsheet,

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:46.860
but also some of us may be
familiar with like ETL tooling

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.250
or other type of
data tooling where

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:53.791
you would describe a flow and
processing steps, in which case

00:06:53.791 --> 00:06:55.290
you're not looking
your data at all.

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:58.230
You're just looking at the
transformations or steps that

00:06:58.230 --> 00:06:59.860
are done to change your data--

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:01.230
equally valid approaches.

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:04.590
But in the case of the
latter, the ETL tooling

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:08.130
or when you see a diagram
with the transformation steps,

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:10.140
it's hard to know exactly
what your data looks

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:11.920
like at any point in time.

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:14.129
You can see the logic
being applied to it,

00:07:14.129 --> 00:07:16.170
but you don't know if that
logic is being applied

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:17.961
in the way you wanted
it, if you're getting

00:07:17.961 --> 00:07:19.230
inputs and outputs expected.

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:22.620
So what we've done is kept
you in kind of the spreadsheet

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:23.567
paradigm.

00:07:23.567 --> 00:07:25.900
So with Dataprep, you're
looking at a grid of your data.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:28.400
But because you can't see all
of your data at the same time,

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:30.240
we've added additional
visual elements

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:33.120
to give you the sense of
the complete data set.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:35.940
So we sample data
so you're looking

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:37.950
at a representative sample.

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:42.030
We apply some visual
distributions atop each column,

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:44.790
give you a sense for the
distribution of columns.

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:48.420
We also detect type and
provide some validation

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:49.495
on those types--

00:07:49.495 --> 00:07:51.870
again, to give you, while you
can't see the whole column,

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:53.820
you can see outliers,
you can see anomalies

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:55.240
using these visual cues.

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:59.040
MARK MANDEL: So talking about
messy data or cleaning data,

00:07:59.040 --> 00:07:59.910
what does that mean?

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:02.430
I just assume that all data
sets are perfect, aren't they?

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:02.760
ERIC ANDERSON: Sure.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:04.830
I mean, it's not like
they're always erroneous,

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:07.200
and data sets might be
fine for one situation

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:10.110
and not so clean or fine
for another situation.

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:13.110
We're all probably familiar
with somewhat erroneous data,

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:16.320
like outliers or anomalies
that aren't representative.

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:18.660
You can imagine
transaction information

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:23.820
and people make returns
or improper transactions.

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:26.070
I mean, using point of sale,
for example, information,

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:28.020
if you wanted to use
that as an example.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:30.680
Not everything that passes
through the [? register ?]

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:33.990
is something that you want to
track perhaps as an analyst.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:36.780
And so, yeah, a part of that
might be cleaning and removing

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:39.659
those erroneous or anomalies.

00:08:39.659 --> 00:08:43.620
But, there might be other things
done as part of preparation.

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:47.300
Enrichment or joining is fairly
obvious and straightforward.

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:51.090
It's very common to have
several different data sources

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:55.210
that you want to combine into
a single, fully-enriched table.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:58.770
Structuring your
data so you have

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:02.670
a single column with a
fully-defined address

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:04.680
but it's hard to filter
on states when they're

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:06.263
embedded in a string,
and so you might

00:09:06.263 --> 00:09:09.660
want to parse out a column
into several different columns,

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:10.960
for example.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:13.470
So you may have
structuring steps.

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:15.510
Aggregations also common.

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:18.500
You have single
records, perhaps,

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:20.850
and you want to create
these into daily totals

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:22.380
or into hourly totals.

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:24.490
These are all things that
can be done in Dataprep.

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:26.114
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
all of these steps,

00:09:26.114 --> 00:09:30.420
though, sound like something
that you could write yourself.

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:33.750
Is Dataprep like a
library of filters

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:37.890
that are useful for
common data science,

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:39.190
or is it more than that?

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:40.710
ERIC ANDERSON: Yeah, certainly.

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:44.270
So the common
alternatives might be SQL.

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:48.000
If this was stored in a
SQL-compliant database

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:52.000
of sorts, you could apply
SQL operations to this.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:54.450
Another alternative,
common one, would be Python

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:56.110
or other scripting language.

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:57.630
There's some great
Python libraries

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:02.130
where you can take things
as a table or frame of data

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:03.559
and apply transformations to it.

00:10:03.559 --> 00:10:05.100
In both of those
cases, again, you're

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:07.660
not able to see the data
itself, and you're not

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:08.910
able to see intermediate data.

00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:10.830
And you're not able
to get kind of a sense

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:13.350
for the full data
set at one time.

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:16.709
There are certain popular
patterns I've used myself,

00:10:16.709 --> 00:10:17.250
I should say.

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:18.791
Data scientists or
data analysts will

00:10:18.791 --> 00:10:22.170
have a cocktail
of common queries

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:25.480
and/or scripts that they'll
throw at a new data set.

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:26.780
Describe the columns.

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:28.750
Show me the distributions
of each column.

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:30.750
So we've kind of packaged
all those common tasks

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:31.860
into a single view.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:35.070
And in addition to just showing
you the view of your data,

00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:37.350
we can then let you
transform it without having

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:38.160
to write scripts.

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:40.440
So if you're very
familiar in Python or SQL,

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:41.640
you might be productive.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.880
But even then, we find
that even Python and SQL

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:47.700
experts find themselves
really productive in Dataprep.

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:49.870
So yeah, in addition to
just viewing your data,

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:52.680
you can also create
transformations, in some case

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:55.710
no-code transformations.

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:57.960
I'll just jump right into
it how that's possible.

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:00.660
We talked about
the distributions

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:03.450
and the anomaly detection we
provide you, those visual cues.

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:06.270
Selecting those visual
cues will select the subset

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:08.620
of data that applies.

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:11.550
And then, Dataprep
will suggest in kind

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:14.640
of like real time
likely transformations

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:15.900
to apply to that subset.

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:19.971
So if we showed you a bunch of
states and 10% of those states

00:11:19.971 --> 00:11:22.470
were not actual states-- they
were territories of the United

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:25.410
States or something--
and you select those,

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:27.060
we might suggest
you remove them,

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:29.700
we might suggest you do
other parsing steps on them.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:31.690
And then you can choose
that transformation.

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:34.260
So you've gone from
explore and understand

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:38.100
my data to remove some anomalies
without writing any code.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:41.434
This has just been like reactive
gestures to the interface.

00:11:41.434 --> 00:11:43.350
MARK MANDEL: So not only
is data prep a GUI so

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:44.860
that you can visualize
your information.

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:47.235
There's also some nice machine
learning behind the scenes

00:11:47.235 --> 00:11:49.950
or something like that to
basically give you suggestions

00:11:49.950 --> 00:11:51.732
on ways you could make
your data possibly

00:11:51.732 --> 00:11:52.690
quote unquote "better?"

00:11:52.690 --> 00:11:53.565
ERIC ANDERSON: Right.

00:11:53.565 --> 00:11:54.120
Yeah.

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:55.869
You're the only judge
of better, but we've

00:11:55.869 --> 00:11:57.870
learned from experience,
or the algorithms

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:00.480
learn from experience,
what is likely to be

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:01.300
interesting to you.

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:02.850
And we serve you
up several options,

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:04.150
and you pick from those.

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:06.608
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So what does
it look like to use Dataprep?

00:12:06.608 --> 00:12:09.240
How do you get the data
into it or out of it?

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:11.760
Because, at the end, it
really feels like you're

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:13.310
basically improving the data.

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:17.100
So I see it as a pipe
of a bigger process.

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:18.900
How do you connect
it to other things?

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:20.816
ERIC ANDERSON: The
integration points for data

00:12:20.816 --> 00:12:23.910
are through GCS and BigQuery.

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:26.910
So we accept all the
standard file formats

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:30.180
you might expect us to on GCS.

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:32.370
And then we accept
BigQuery tables.

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:33.890
We'd like to expand
that over time,

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:36.900
but we've actually found
that this solves the 90% use

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:39.180
case for GCP users.

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:41.724
MARK MANDEL: Is this
like a one-time thing,

00:12:41.724 --> 00:12:43.140
or is this something
that could be

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:45.635
put in a pipeline of
ongoing streaming data?

00:12:45.635 --> 00:12:47.760
ERIC ANDERSON: I think what
we want to solve really

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:51.390
well initially is
necessarily the experience,

00:12:51.390 --> 00:12:53.250
and we're delivering on that.

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:55.600
And that's something
that's been exciting to us.

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:57.630
But as soon as you
solve something one time

00:12:57.630 --> 00:13:01.734
as an analyst or as a scientist
or whoever, most people,

00:13:01.734 --> 00:13:03.900
they want to schedule that
thing to run all the time

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:06.190
or check that into production.

00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:09.210
And so we are in the process
of adding those features.

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:11.160
Scheduling is soon
to arrive to allow

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:14.934
you to schedule that execution
to happen on some time basis.

00:13:14.934 --> 00:13:17.100
And then, eventually, we
want to support the ability

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:19.926
to programmatically execute
those transformations

00:13:19.926 --> 00:13:20.550
by other means.

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:21.466
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:21.466 --> 00:13:23.670
So I was watching a
video on Dataprep,

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:25.090
and it looks really cool.

00:13:25.090 --> 00:13:27.510
And now, I get a little
bit more there of it

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:30.120
really looks like a
super-powered spreadsheet.

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:31.687
It's really cool.

00:13:31.687 --> 00:13:33.270
But also, one of the
things that I saw

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:37.717
was, when you click on Run
Job, you see Cloud Dataflow.

00:13:37.717 --> 00:13:38.550
ERIC ANDERSON: Yeah.

00:13:38.550 --> 00:13:40.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What is the
relationship between Cloud

00:13:40.070 --> 00:13:41.370
Dataflow and Cloud Dataprep?

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:41.640
ERIC ANDERSON: Great.

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:42.550
Yeah, great question.

00:13:42.550 --> 00:13:44.424
We're pretty excited
about that relationship.

00:13:44.424 --> 00:13:46.110
So for those of
you who don't know,

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:50.490
Dataflow is a massively-parallel
data processing service

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:52.600
and/or framework depending
on how you look at it.

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:55.710
And so it's very
powerful, and it's

00:13:55.710 --> 00:13:57.420
based on technology
we've been developing

00:13:57.420 --> 00:13:58.670
a Google for a long time--

00:13:58.670 --> 00:14:00.520
MapReduce, Flume, et cetera.

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:03.260
So to have Dataflow at
your fingertips is awesome.

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:06.570
But a lot of people are ready to
use Dataflow through it's also

00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:08.880
expressive and powerful APIs.

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:11.040
Currently, Java and
Python are supported.

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:13.770
And Dataprep, what
it allows you to do

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:17.190
is to express these things in
an intuitive, visual way using

00:14:17.190 --> 00:14:18.300
Dataprep.

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:20.190
And then, you can know
that, even though I'm

00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:23.810
having a great spreadsheet
experience, behind the scenes

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:26.550
I'm going to have also an
awesome, massively-parallel,

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:29.010
powerful processing experience.

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:31.835
So when you get Run
Job, we take the logic

00:14:31.835 --> 00:14:34.320
that you've generated
on the sample using

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:36.900
either predicted transformations
or your own custom

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:37.740
transformations.

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:40.410
We package that logic
and pass it to Dataflow,

00:14:40.410 --> 00:14:43.230
and Dataflow creates a job
that reads from your source

00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:46.170
and writes to your destination
with all the processing

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:47.367
logic you defined.

00:14:47.367 --> 00:14:49.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
it feels like, once you

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:52.380
have that running, Cloud
Dataflow one of the cool things

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:54.720
is that you can run
it as a batch process,

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:57.120
or you can also
run it as something

00:14:57.120 --> 00:14:58.329
that is continuously running.

00:14:58.329 --> 00:14:59.745
And as soon as you
get some input,

00:14:59.745 --> 00:15:01.290
you get the output
on the other side.

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:04.090
Is that possible
with Cloud Dataprep?

00:15:04.090 --> 00:15:06.170
Like, you would basically
be fine continuously

00:15:06.170 --> 00:15:09.280
to running Dataflow job
and then you can just

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:12.340
send things into the input
and get the clean version

00:15:12.340 --> 00:15:13.200
on the other side?

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:14.033
ERIC ANDERSON: Yeah.

00:15:14.033 --> 00:15:16.330
Continuously running is
something that's never really

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:19.570
been done in the frame of
data preparation industry,

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:21.320
if you want to call
it that, or landscape.

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:24.070
Data preparation has always
been kind of a batch thing.

00:15:24.070 --> 00:15:26.770
What's neat about where
we're at today with Dataprep,

00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:28.750
yes, it's batch only today.

00:15:28.750 --> 00:15:30.910
But it is, because
we're running Dataflow,

00:15:30.910 --> 00:15:33.640
we are submitting
Beam pipelines.

00:15:33.640 --> 00:15:35.855
And the Beam programming
model allows both batch

00:15:35.855 --> 00:15:36.700
and streaming.

00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:38.320
So it would be a
natural evolution

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:40.532
for us to go into
streaming in the future,

00:15:40.532 --> 00:15:42.740
and that's something I think
would be super exciting.

00:15:42.740 --> 00:15:45.115
And we've heard some requests
for that from our users.

00:15:45.115 --> 00:15:46.510
MARK MANDEL: So
actually, that probably

00:15:46.510 --> 00:15:47.740
segues into a nice question.

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:52.060
When should a customer choose
either Dataprep or Dataflow?

00:15:52.060 --> 00:15:53.950
It sounds like there
is crossover there.

00:15:53.950 --> 00:15:56.120
Streaming seems like an obvious
choice because of Dataflow.

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:57.540
But are there other
features there

00:15:57.540 --> 00:15:59.740
that may make someone
choose one or the other?

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:00.190
ERIC ANDERSON: Yeah, sure.

00:16:00.190 --> 00:16:02.970
So I'll start with some of the
obvious, if you want call them,

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:04.070
limitations.

00:16:04.070 --> 00:16:07.900
One way to think about it is
that Dataflow kind of just

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:09.400
supports almost anything.

00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:14.172
It's a programming interface so
that you can almost do anything

00:16:14.172 --> 00:16:15.880
with Dataflow, even
things that maybe you

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:17.260
shouldn't do with Dataflow.

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:20.800
Where Dataprep provides
you, it's constrained.

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:24.370
I should describe that we have
your transformations create

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:28.090
kind of script steps, and we
use a human-readable script

00:16:28.090 --> 00:16:32.490
within Dataprep that then is
translated into the Beam SDK.

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:34.790
So yeah, because we're
using a DSL on Beam,

00:16:34.790 --> 00:16:38.300
certainly we're constrained
to the limits of that DSL,

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:40.300
which is fairly expressive
and we actually think

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:41.650
is more expressive than SQL.

00:16:41.650 --> 00:16:44.090
So you should have plenty
of room to grow there.

00:16:44.090 --> 00:16:45.970
But yeah, currently
today, for example,

00:16:45.970 --> 00:16:48.850
you couldn't pass
arbitrary code to Dataprep.

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:51.490
For that type of use, you
want to bounce out to Dataflow

00:16:51.490 --> 00:16:53.170
and do everything there.

00:16:53.170 --> 00:16:55.845
It is possible to, of course,
chain these into a workflow.

00:16:55.845 --> 00:16:57.220
Because we're
reading and writing

00:16:57.220 --> 00:17:00.030
from common sources,
GCS and BigQuery,

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:02.130
you could combine
Dataflow and Dataprep

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:04.730
into sequenced pipelines.

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:06.089
But more of the comparison.

00:17:06.089 --> 00:17:10.119
So if you prefer a
visual environment,

00:17:10.119 --> 00:17:13.069
if you prefer a human-readable
script language,

00:17:13.069 --> 00:17:14.569
Dataprep is the
right place for you.

00:17:14.569 --> 00:17:18.190
If you prefer the expressiveness
of a programming language

00:17:18.190 --> 00:17:21.280
and/or maybe some of
the like data engineery

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:23.829
processes around a
programming language--

00:17:23.829 --> 00:17:25.810
if you want write unit
tests, if you want

00:17:25.810 --> 00:17:27.619
to write other kinds of tests--

00:17:27.619 --> 00:17:29.230
Dataflow and a
programing language

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:30.730
is the right place for you.

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:34.240
MARK MANDEL: Is there any
limitation on what sort of data

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:35.679
formats Dataprep can handle?

00:17:35.679 --> 00:17:36.970
Obviously, you've got BigQuery.

00:17:36.970 --> 00:17:40.967
But if you said GCS, I could
put anything I want into GCS.

00:17:40.967 --> 00:17:41.800
ERIC ANDERSON: Yeah.

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:44.200
So we take text formats.

00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:47.260
We're going to be able
to like open most things,

00:17:47.260 --> 00:17:49.810
but we're limited to a
handful of formats in which we

00:17:49.810 --> 00:17:51.340
understand the structure.

00:17:51.340 --> 00:17:56.180
So like Avro, CSV, JSON,
these are all supported.

00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:57.070
There's a few others.

00:17:57.070 --> 00:17:58.990
Actually, Excel files
are also supported.

00:17:58.990 --> 00:18:02.120
We should add Sheets to that
list, but it's not there yet.

00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:04.210
And then, I was going
to mention, for example,

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:06.460
XML isn't supported,
although it's

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:09.760
similar to JSON in a
semi-structured file format.

00:18:09.760 --> 00:18:12.220
You'll be able to
read XML, but it

00:18:12.220 --> 00:18:14.590
won't come in as a structured
format like the same way

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:16.100
that we did JSON, for example.

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:16.850
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:18:16.850 --> 00:18:18.640
So what I would love
to know is, if people

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:20.890
are interested in Dataprep
or they seem like this

00:18:20.890 --> 00:18:22.514
is interesting, what's
a great resource

00:18:22.514 --> 00:18:24.040
they can go to
learn more about it?

00:18:24.040 --> 00:18:24.873
ERIC ANDERSON: Sure.

00:18:24.873 --> 00:18:26.570
And I should also
give you at some point

00:18:26.570 --> 00:18:28.802
like where we're at in
our timeline and launch

00:18:28.802 --> 00:18:29.760
and that sort of thing.

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:32.290
Resources, so we
have a quickstart

00:18:32.290 --> 00:18:34.830
available and docs available.

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:37.480
The quickstart is like we
give you some sample data

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:39.770
and suggest some
transformations.

00:18:39.770 --> 00:18:42.970
If you're familiar with the
Google Cloud Platform Codelabs,

00:18:42.970 --> 00:18:45.290
there's actually
already a couple set up.

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:48.102
Some of them are designed
for using Dataprep

00:18:48.102 --> 00:18:49.810
in the context of
machine learning, which

00:18:49.810 --> 00:18:52.150
is something we can discuss.

00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:54.854
And there have been a
couple blogs out there.

00:18:54.854 --> 00:18:55.645
It's still nascent.

00:18:55.645 --> 00:18:57.353
I'm going to go ahead
and jump into where

00:18:57.353 --> 00:18:59.170
we're at kind of our rollout.

00:18:59.170 --> 00:19:01.660
We announced
Dataprep, that we were

00:19:01.660 --> 00:19:07.120
working on this, originally in
the spring at Google Cloud Next

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:08.110
2017.

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:10.600
And it was then
in what we called

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:14.050
a private alpha release for some
time, or private beta release.

00:19:14.050 --> 00:19:16.180
And then, we opened
it up to a public beta

00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:17.275
just a few weeks ago.

00:19:17.275 --> 00:19:19.540
So now, anybody--
all you listening--

00:19:19.540 --> 00:19:21.760
can go find it in
the Cloud Console

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:23.260
among all the other products.

00:19:23.260 --> 00:19:25.676
What you should look forward
to is some additional feature

00:19:25.676 --> 00:19:27.370
releases and a
general availability

00:19:27.370 --> 00:19:28.540
at some point in the future.

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:29.360
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:31.305
You mentioned Dataprep
and machine learning.

00:19:31.305 --> 00:19:32.680
Machine learning
is the hot word,

00:19:32.680 --> 00:19:35.330
so what's the connection there?

00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:38.867
ERIC ANDERSON: Just what add
as many buzzwords as possible.

00:19:38.867 --> 00:19:40.450
Often, we think about
data preparation

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:43.840
in the context of business
intelligence or kind

00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:45.070
of data analyst work.

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:47.200
In which case, a
lot of that work,

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:48.910
it can be plumbing,
kind of like ETL.

00:19:48.910 --> 00:19:50.930
It can also be a little
more higher level,

00:19:50.930 --> 00:19:52.120
like you're trying to
arrive at some answers

00:19:52.120 --> 00:19:54.350
and you're preparing a
data set for those answers.

00:19:54.350 --> 00:19:56.470
But the analysis is
fairly straightforward

00:19:56.470 --> 00:19:58.330
using straightforward
tools downstream--

00:19:58.330 --> 00:20:01.210
data visualization,
simple aggregations.

00:20:01.210 --> 00:20:04.620
In the case of maybe more
advanced machine and data

00:20:04.620 --> 00:20:09.060
science, even those that are
very comfortable in Python

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:12.000
or whatever common machine
learning or data science

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:13.980
interface you prefer,
we've actually

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:17.102
found some people that prefer
to jump over Dataprep to do

00:20:17.102 --> 00:20:18.060
their data preparation.

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:21.095
So in a machine learning
data science workflow,

00:20:21.095 --> 00:20:24.480
you're going to take
these multiple data sets

00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:26.565
and you're going to
combine them in some way.

00:20:26.565 --> 00:20:28.440
And then, you're going
to do some exploration

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:30.600
to make sure that you
don't have too much noise

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:31.720
or anomalous data.

00:20:31.720 --> 00:20:34.600
And you're also going to
do what we [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:36.090
feature engineering.

00:20:36.090 --> 00:20:39.027
You're going to make some
judgment about what you think

00:20:39.027 --> 00:20:40.860
are the important
features, and you're going

00:20:40.860 --> 00:20:42.610
to put those in unique columns.

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:45.450
And then, you might
normalize those columns all

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:48.540
in preparation for then applying
you're kind of out-of-the-box

00:20:48.540 --> 00:20:49.560
algorithms.

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:52.380
That upstream work around
combining the data set

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:53.620
can be done in Dataprep.

00:20:53.620 --> 00:20:55.620
The feature engineering
work, in fact-- actually

00:20:55.620 --> 00:20:57.995
separating out the important
features into unique columns

00:20:57.995 --> 00:21:00.300
and normalizing them--
can be done in Dataprep.

00:21:00.300 --> 00:21:03.660
If you're familiar with Kaggle,
which is this machine learning

00:21:03.660 --> 00:21:05.980
and data science kind of
competition and forum,

00:21:05.980 --> 00:21:11.270
community, one of the big Kaggle
users who's won a few contests

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:14.000
was super excited about
Dataprep and has been using it

00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:15.510
in his Kaggle work.

00:21:15.510 --> 00:21:18.750
So there's some
interesting work there.

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:20.640
Just to touch on the
workflow, these people

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:23.430
are probably doing
initial work in Python,

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:28.290
realizing that they have some
data issues or strange behavior

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:29.250
in their algorithms.

00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:33.290
And they might bounce out to
Dataprep using GCS or BigQuery

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:34.860
as the interchange point.

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:37.980
And they can go back to
their Python environment,

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:41.161
for example, and pick up the
new data sets that are cleaned.

00:21:41.161 --> 00:21:41.910
